---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Passage = `THE CAMPUS WIRELESS

A college replaces its wireless network. The old one
used a single password printed on posters around the
building. The new one requires each student to sign in
with their college account and a code sent to their
phone.

The network keeps a record of every device that
connects: the account it signed in with, which access
point it connected through, and the time. Access points
are spread across the campus, so the record shows
roughly where a device was and for how long.

The college publishes weekly building-occupancy figures
from this data, with accounts removed, so departments
can plan room bookings. One department asked for the
figures broken down by hour and by individual room. The
smallest rooms hold four people.

Traffic to the college's own systems is encrypted.
Traffic to other sites is whatever those sites use. A
notice on the sign-in page says the college does not
inspect the content of traffic it does not host.

Some students report seeing a second network with a
name almost identical to the college's, which does not
ask for an account. It has appeared near the library
several times.

The old posters with the shared password have not all
been taken down.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Question Set 23: Reading Passage</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">
          Five questions &middot; one passage &middot; 12 minutes
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b>The third reading-passage set in the course, and the last before
            Quarter 4.</b> Five of the seventy exam questions share one passage about a
            computing innovation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This one draws on the whole of the last eight sessions</b> &mdash;
            privacy, aggregation, authentication, encryption, and unauthorized access.
            None of it is named in the passage.
          </p>

          <h4>How to Do This One</h4>

          <ul>
            <li><b>Read the whole passage first.</b> All of it, before any question.</li>
            <li><b>Set a timer for 12 minutes.</b> The reading is part of it.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Every answer is in the passage or follows from it.</b> Nothing needs
              outside knowledge about colleges or networking hardware.
            </li>
            <li>Mark each answer <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, as always.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="passage">
          <h2>The Passage</h2>

          <Code
            code={Passage}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Read this before looking at the questions"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="questions">
          <h2>The Questions</h2>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 1</h3>

          <p>
            The new sign-in requires a college account and a code sent to a phone. This
            is best described as:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) two-step verification using two pieces of knowledge</li>
            <li>(B) multifactor authentication using knowledge and possession</li>
            <li>(C) encryption of the sign-in process</li>
            <li>(D) a certificate authority validating the student's identity</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 2</h3>

          <p>
            The college publishes occupancy figures with accounts removed. The
            department wants them broken down <b>by hour and by individual room</b>, and
            the smallest rooms hold four people. Which is the strongest objection?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) The figures would be less accurate at that level of detail</li>
            <li>(B) Encryption does not protect data once it has been published</li>
            <li>(C) Removing accounts does not prevent identification, because a small room at a specific hour may correspond to one person</li>
            <li>(D) The college has no legitimate reason to collect location data at all</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 3 &mdash; Select all that apply</h3>

          <p>
            Which are supported by the passage as <b>risks created by the new
            system</b>?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) The network records roughly where each device was and for how long</li>
            <li>(B) A second network with a nearly identical name has appeared near the library</li>
            <li>(C) Traffic to sites the college does not host may not be encrypted</li>
            <li>(D) Posters showing the old shared password are still displayed</li>
            <li>(E) Requiring a phone code makes accounts easier to compromise</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 4</h3>

          <p>
            A student connects to the second network, the one with the almost-identical
            name that does not ask for an account. What is this most likely an example
            of, and what is the main risk?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) A rogue access point &mdash; data sent over it can be intercepted, analyzed, and modified</li>
            <li>(B) Phishing &mdash; the student is being tricked into providing personal information</li>
            <li>(C) A computer virus &mdash; connecting will copy malicious software onto the device</li>
            <li>(D) Keylogging &mdash; the network will record everything the student types</li>
          </ul>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Question 5</h3>

          <p>
            The college states that it does not inspect the content of traffic to sites
            it does not host. Which statement is best supported?
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>(A) That traffic is therefore private, since the college is the only party with access to the network</li>
            <li>(B) The statement is about what the college does, and does not by itself make that traffic unreadable to anyone else</li>
            <li>(C) Encrypting traffic to the college's own systems also protects traffic to other sites</li>
            <li>(D) The record of which access point a device used reveals the content of that device's traffic</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Five answers, each marked <b>sure</b> or <b>unsure</b>, plus how far you got
            when the timer went off. Worth <b>5 points</b>, graded on completion.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>And one written answer, in a sentence or two:</b> the college removed
            accounts from the published figures and believed that made them anonymous.
            Name the idea from session 75 that explains why that is not enough.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Start the Timer
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
